   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "The base branch's test check fails with a confinement failure. The implementer's first prompt says that check is 'already FAILING on the base branch (not your fault, fix only if in scope)'. If the implementer then leaves it failing, the round is blocked as a new failure, which can waste an implementation attempt.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test $TMPDIR/adj/r.test.ts  (implementPrompt with baseline check {ok:false, confinementError:true})",
    "output": "- test: `bun test` \u2014 already FAILING on the base branch (not your fault, fix only if in scope)\n(and compareGates for a failing confined candidate against that baseline gives \"new_failure\" blocking=true)"
   },
   "evidence": "Head src/pipeline/prompts.ts:117-120: `const note = base && !base.ok ? \" \u2014 already FAILING on the base branch (not your fault, fix only if in scope)\" : \"\";` does not check confinementError. Head run.ts:348: `else if (!before || baselineConfinementError) verdict = r.ok ? \"new_pass\" : \"new_failure\";` makes that failure blocking. At base the check was always blocked as a confinement error whatever the implementer did, so the misleading text had no effect there. This change makes the outcome depend on what the implementer does, so the text now matters. The request only asked for the blocking feedback reason, which formatGateFeedback does deliver, so this is not a requirement gap."
  },
